{x}^{2}" 13194139534829 true] [17592186312849 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534829 true] [17592186312849 1111 #uuid "0f4ee260-3bc9-5400-bf56-47d9322bb48f" 13194139534829 true] [17592186312850 1017 #edb/ref 17592186312851 13194139534829 true] [17592186312850 1089 "q-161622" 13194139534829 true] [17592186312850 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534829 true] [17592186312850 1100 "Consider the set $A,$ defined by \n\n$$\nA={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,y\\ge -1+x}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A$ contains all of its boundary points.\n- $A$ contains none of its boundary points.\n- $\\text{int}\\,A=A$" 13194139534829 true] [17592186312850 1113 #uuid "157d4457-1f9d-56e1-8728-9f512b3e63e5" 13194139534829 true] [17592186312850 1145 "The boundary of a set $A,$ denoted $∂A,$ is the set that contains all the boundary points of $A.$\n\nIn this case, the set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041552/assets/3e7d8c11ac4b3de8473741a362ad2b78555c764da2d61f955435e8d59771cf46.png)\n\nThe interior of a set $A,$ denoted $\\text{int}\\,A,$ is the set containing all the interior points of $A.$ It contains every point in $A$ except the boundary points: \n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A=A-∂A\n$$\n\nNotice that, in this case, the set $A$ contains all of its boundary points. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. As we've seen, $A$ contains all of its boundary points.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $A$ contains all of its boundary points, we must have $A-∂A⊊A.$ Therefore $\\text{int}\\,A\\ne A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139534829 true] [17592186312851 1000 #edb/ref 17592186312852 13194139534829 true] [17592186312851 1000 #edb/ref 17592186312853 13194139534829 true] [17592186312851 1000 #edb/ref 17592186312854 13194139534829 true] [17592186312851 1000 #edb/ref