312857 1145 "The boundary of a set $A,$ denoted $∂A,$ is the set that contains all the boundary points of $A.$\n\nIn this case, the set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041552/assets/cdca164d343d8bff4c78a1e366e52c588ca6c54dfdea61acea1900bcbdc79f31.png)\n\nThe interior of a set $A,$ denoted $\\text{int}\\,A,$ is the set containing all the interior points of $A.$ It contains every point in $A$ except the boundary points: \n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A=A-∂A\n$$\n\nNotice that, in this case, the set $A$ contains all of its boundary points.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statements I and II are false. As we have seen, $A$ contains all its boundary points.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $A$ contains all of its boundary points, we must have $A-∂A⊊A.$ Therefore $\\text{int}\\,A\\ne A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139534829 true] [17592186312858 1000 #edb/ref 17592186312859 13194139534829 true] [17592186312858 1000 #edb/ref 17592186312860 13194139534829 true] [17592186312858 1000 #edb/ref 17592186312861 13194139534829 true] [17592186312858 1000 #edb/ref 17592186312862 13194139534829 true] [17592186312858 1000 #edb/ref 17592186312863 13194139534829 true] [17592186312858 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534829 true] [17592186312858 1041 "selection" 13194139534829 true] [17592186312858 1104 #edb/ref 17592186312861 13194139534829 true] [17592186312858 1110 #uuid "61119d38-e53f-549e-8495-e7db2ce3039a" 13194139534829 true] [17592186312859 1034 "None of the statements are true" 13194139534829 true] [17592186312859 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534829 true] [17592186312859 1111 #uuid "24911b0e-c9d5-5861-b5ff-fd0fcb4ff12f" 13194139534829 true] [17592186312860 1034 "I and III only" 13194139534829 true] [17592186312860 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534829 true] [17592186312860 1111 #uuid "4982bc7f-6393-550c-8019-d27e45da89c3" 13194139534829 true] [17592186312861 1034 "III only" 13194139534829 true] [17592186312861 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534829 true] [17592186312861 1111 #uuid "8d020131-8ca5-5ac2-93bb-296db0e835a2" 13194139534829 true] [17592186312862 1034 "I and II only" 13194139534829 true] [17592186312862 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534829 true] [17592186312862 1111 #uuid "b06acb3c-1b5f-5e4b-acd5-3d88191085f0" 13194139534829 true] [17592186312863 1034 "II only" 13194139534829 true] [17592186312863 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534829 true] [17592186312863 1111 #uuid "2cb0c4b8-2fb2-5c9c-9594-9a0f3269b936" 13194139534829 true] [17592186312864 1017 #edb/ref 17592186312865 13194139534829 true] [17592186312864 1089 "q-161626" 13194139534829 true] [17592186312864 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534829 true] [17592186312864 1100 "Consider the set $A,$ defined by \n\n$$\nA={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+{y}^{2}<4}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\text{int}\\,A\\cap ∂A=∅$\n- $A-\\text{int}\\,A=∂A$\n- $∂A⊂{R}^{2}-A$" 13194139534829 true] [17592186312864 1113 #uuid "81147b8a-c890-53be-ba74-33cc71183412" 13194139534829 true] [17592186312864 1145 "The boundary of a set $A,$ denoted $∂A,$ is the set that contains all the boundary points of $A.$\n\nIn this case, the set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041552/assets/4540e03fc5404474a0cd900c89004e6a5cfe7ac2d4702348e9d0395fc1c914e9.png)\n\n \n\nThe interior of a set $A,$ denoted $\\text{int}\\,A,$ is the set containing all the interior points of $A.$ It contains every point in $A$ except the boundary points: \n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A=A-∂A\n$$\n\nThe interior of $A$ is shown below.  \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041552/assets/2a93b912f0d7b3a84bf0ec9c3b3596d080a9e71a345ff436e2f591edf042d9db.png)\n\n  \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $\\text{int}\\,A$ contains all points in $A$ except the boundary points, we must have $\\text{int}\\,A\\cap ∂A=∅,$ where $∅$ denotes the empty set.\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $A$ contains none of its boundary points, we must have $A=\\text{int}\\,A$ or, equivalently,  $A-\\text{int}\\,A=∅.$\n\n- Statement III is true. In this case, the set $A$ contains none of its boundary points, so we must have $∂A⊂{R}^{2}-A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and  III only.\"" 13194139534829 true] [17592186312865 1000 #edb/ref 17592186312866 13194139534829 true] [17592186312865 1000 #edb/ref 17592186312867 13194139534829 true] [17592186312865 1000 #edb/ref 17592186312868 13194139534829 true] [17592186312865 1000 #edb/ref 17592186312869 13194139534829 true] [17592186312865 1000 #edb/ref 17592186312870 13194139534829 true] [17592186312865 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534829 true] [17592186312865 1041 "selection" 13194139534829 true] [17592186312865 1104 #edb/ref 17592186312866 13194139534829 true] [17592186312865 1110 #uuid "01680378-49dc-5671-b5de-e62d50e41efd" 13194139534829 true] [17592186312866 1034 "I and III only" 13194139534829 true] [17592186312866 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534829 true] [17592186312866 1111 #uuid "c661fcfe-c80e-57aa-985a-513b1f79f913" 13194139534829 true] [17592186312867 1034 "I only" 13194139534829 true] [17592186312867 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534829 true] [17592186312867 1111 #uuid "e87ff6e8-d276-588e-8bfd-078bb9d41f3c" 13194139534829 true] [17592186312868 1034 "III only" 13194139534829 true] [17592186312868 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534829 true] [17592186312868 1111 #uuid "b998510f-38fa-54a4-9474-b1cec36cded4" 13194139534829 true] [17592186312869 1034 "I and II only" 13194139534829 true] [17592186312869 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534829 true] [17592186312869 1111 #uuid "de9378